From c3b4ccaca3f0522dff1f9f11a4efadb0f9a8581d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Noah Evans <noah.evans@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 08:51:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] hw/misc: model the BCM2835 SMI, replacing the
 unimplemented stub

SMI_OFFSET was create_unimp(), so a guest could not reach the secondary
memory interface at all.

Modelled from the Raspberry Pi kernel's
include/linux/broadcom/bcm2835_smi.h, which is the only complete public
description of this block: it documents what every bit does, not merely
what it is called.  Upstream Linux has no driver for it.  Both register
sets are covered -- direct mode's SMIDCS/SMIDA/SMIDD, and programmed
mode's SMICS/SMIL/SMIA/SMID with the FIFO -- along with all four device
timing banks, the DMA request thresholds, the interrupt enables, and
the FIFO debug counters.

There is no external device, and there cannot be one: SMI's whole
purpose is the far side of its pins, and QEMU has no abstraction for a
parallel memory-mapped peripheral to attach.  Unlike the AUX SPI in
this series, which borrows QEMU's own m25p80, this is PWM tier -- reads
return zeroes, writes are discarded, no signal reaches a pin.

What is modelled, and is the reason it is worth having, is the timing.
A word takes setup + strobe + hold + pace cycles of the SMI clock,
taken from the clock manager's smi mux, exactly as the device settings
registers say.  That turns those registers from storage a driver writes
and never sees again into something with an observable consequence.

Two behaviours worth calling out because they are what a driver gets
wrong.  DONE is asymmetric: for a write it is set when the last word
has gone, and for a read not until the FIFO has also been emptied, so a
driver that waits on DONE before reading waits forever.  And SMIFD's
counters are six bits, so they saturate at 63 and cannot express a full
64-word FIFO -- a driver that drains exactly FCNT words leaves one
behind.

Two bugs found while writing the guest driver against it.  The
completion test only ran on a sync that had moved a word, so DONE was
never owed to the later access that drained the FIFO.  And a read whose
words had all moved but whose FIFO was still full stayed ACTIVE with
nothing scheduled to change that, so rescheduling armed a deadline
already in the past and livelocked the main loop; nothing is scheduled
once the last word has moved.

